 "approvalMessage": {
  "approvalNumber": 34,
  "billId": {
   "basePrintNo": "A2111",
   "basePrintNoStr": "A2111-2021",
   "printNo": "A2111",
   "session": 2021,
   "version": ""
  },
  "chapter": 589,
  "signer": "",
  "text": " \n                APPROVAL MEMORANDUM - No. 34 Chapter 589\n \n        MEMORANDUM filed with Senate Bill Number 2981, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the education law and the state finance law, in\n        relation to creating a firearm violence research institute\"\n \n       MEMORANDUM filed with Assembly Bill Number 2111, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend the tax law,  in  relation  to  providing  taxpayer\n        gifts  for gun violence research; and to amend the state finance\n        law, in relation to establishing the gun violence research fund\"\n \n    APPROVED\n \n  These bills create a Firearm Violence Research Institute (the  \"Insti-\ntute\") within the State Education Department and a gun violence research\nincome  tax donation to assist with funding gun violence research in New\nYork. The Research Institute is charged  with  researching  all  matters\nrelated  to  gun  violence and advising the governor, governmental agen-\ncies, the regents, and the legislature on matters  relating  to  firearm\nviolence in New York State.\n \n  S2981  sets  forth  the  members  of the Institute, including relevant\nstate agency commissioners and appointees of the  Senate  and  Assembly,\nand  establishes  a scientific committee to be made up of experts in the\nfield of gun violence. The bill also establishes a special revenue  fund\nto provide a mechanism for funding this research, although the bill does\nnot provide actual funding for the Institute.\n \n  Gun  violence  is  a  scourge on our state and our nation. Starting in\n1996, the federal government  specifically  prohibited  the  expense  of\nfederal  funds on gun violence research, presumably at the demand of the\ngun sales lobby. To counter this dangerous action,  in  2018,  New  York\njoined with 7 other states to create the Gun Violence Research Consorti-\num. Directed by the Nelson A. Rockefeller Institute of Government at the\nState  University  of  New  York  (\"SUNY\")  and  national experts on gun\nviolence research, the Consortium has conducted many public  discussions\non  the  issue  of  gun  violence  and  produced  a plethora of valuable\nresearch reports covering gun  violence  issues.  This  expert  research\nhelps  us  to  understand the root causes and impacts of gun violence on\nour society, and potential solutions to address it.\n \n  In conversations with the Legislature, it became clear that,  although\nthe  bill  was drafted to house the Institute within the State Education\nDepartment and the Regents, the intent was to ensure that SUNY's work on\nthis issue continue to be supported  and  expanded.  Therefore,  I  have\nsecured  an  agreement  with  the  Legislature to position the Institute\nwithin SUNY at the Nelson A. Rockefeller Institute of Government.  Addi-\ntionally, we have agreed that the Institute will retain  flexibility  to\nensure  its research can delve into all aspects of gun violence that may\nbecome relevant in the future.\n \n  In addition to S2981, the Legislature passed A2111,  which  creates  a\nfunding  source  to  assist gun violence research, as well as a separate\ngun violence research fund. The bill as drafted would create a competing\nresearch fund to that of S2981, and would not intuitively be utilized by\nthe State's newly-created Firearm Violence Research  Institute,  I  have\n \ntherefore  secured an agreement with the Legislature to direct the funds\ncollected through the income tax donation  created  by  A2111  into  the\nfirearm violence research fund created by S2981, to go toward support of\nthe  research  that  will  be conducted by the Firearm Violence Research\nInstitute. These two important pieces of legislation will work  together\ncohesively to build the State's infrastructure in this imperative initi-\native. On the basis of the agreement with the Legislature to amend these\nbills  to  ensure  they  can be implemented effectively, I am pleased to\nsign these bills.\n \n  The bills are approved.                        (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                                ______\n",
